<DocsLayout title="Chudware documentation" description="Install the desktop app, build your first part, then work through modeling, assemblies, simulation, CAM, PLM, reverse engineering, and the copilot." section="Start here">
  <h2>Build something real</h2>
  <p>The guides follow the shape of a real project: get set up, sketch and constrain geometry, assemble parts, rebuild from scans or photos, prepare CAM, and keep the project traceable.</p>
  <p>First session? Start with the <a href="/docs/install-windows/">Windows</a>, <a href="/docs/install-linux/">Linux</a>, or <a href="/docs/install-macos/">macOS</a> install guide, then continue to <a href="/docs/first-part/">your first part</a>. To build Chudware yourself, follow <a href="https://github.com/Hunter-124/Chudware#building-from-source">Building from source</a> in the repository README.</p>
  <h2>Rebuild from source evidence</h2>
  <p>The <a href="/docs/reverse-engineering/">reverse engineering guide</a> covers photos, video, scan meshes, datasheets, and library records, with explicit scale confirmation, dimension review, approximate-result labels, deviation, and provenance.</p>
  <h2>Use the copilot as a collaborator</h2>
  <p>The copilot is a team of agents that can inspect and change the open design, but it does not replace engineering judgment. Review the feature tree, measurements, and highlighted changes before accepting the result.</p>
  <h2>Keep help close</h2>
  <p>Browse the guides by topic or search for a tool, command, or workflow.</p>
</DocsLayout>
